From b48646a565337c920c5ba36be235a74db1f4ad19 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dimitry B <dimitryB@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 15:30:45 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] eip155: a stalled RPC can neither hold a settlement nor lose
 one

Since a transaction goes to one RPC at a time, a node that accepts the
connection and never answers held the send with no limit: Http had no
timeout, and the sequential fallback moves on only on a transport
error. Reads race, and the loser of a race records nothing, so a
stalled RPC stayed the first choice. /settle never answered, and when
the socket finally dropped the detached settlement went on and paid
after the client had left. The next settlements hung behind it.

Every RPC request now ends after 10 s, which counts as unreachable: the
send moves on, and the failure counts against that RPC's rank. alloy
scores the last ten results, so the rank flips after a failure or two.

That alone would bring back the failure the one-RPC send removed. An
RPC can forward the transaction and only then stall, and the next RPC
rejects the copy, so a mined settlement would be reported as failed. So
the transaction is signed before the send, its hash is known, and a
failed send asks the chain for that hash. If an RPC knows it, the
settlement carries on from the hash, and the nonce is kept. If none
does, the send failed as before.

An RPC that took the transaction, stalled, and whose copy has not yet
reached the node answering the lookup is still reported as failed; that
is the ambiguous send already listed as a limit.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
